@startuml SCRUM-400 Hybrid Recommendation Pipeline

title Personalised Recommendations — Candidate Generation, Re-ranking and Feedback (SCRUM-400)

actor "Visitor\n(guest or buyer)" as U
participant "Browser\n(Home.jsx / AuctionCard.jsx)" as B
participant "SecurityFilter\n/*" as SF
participant "RecommendationApiServlet\nGET /api/recommendations" as S
participant "RecommendationDAO" as DAO
participant "UserBasedCollaborativeFilter" as UBCF
database "PostgreSQL\n(bids, watchlist, browse_history,\nauction, recommendation_settings,\nrecommendation_events)" as DB

== Landing page requests recommendations ==

U -> B : Open /
B -> SF : GET /api/recommendations?limit=8
note right of SF
  SecurityFilter (/*) adds security
  headers only — no auth. The route is
  PUBLIC: a guest still gets a strip,
  but never a personalised one.
end note
activate SF
SF -> S : chain
deactivate SF

activate S
S -> DAO : getSettings()
activate DAO
alt cached snapshot older than 30 s
    DAO -> DB : SELECT key, value FROM recommendation_settings
    DB --> DAO : 14 rows
    DAO -> DAO : clamp each value, cache for 30 s
end
DAO --> S : Settings (itemsShown=8, weights, τ, windows, divisor)
deactivate DAO
S -> S : limit = clamp(request limit, 1..24), default = settings.itemsShown
S -> S : userId = sessionUserId(req)\n(null for a guest; forced null on /trending)

alt guest — no session user
    note right of S
      <b>Unregistered visitor</b>: no personalised
      stage runs at all. trending() is a plain
      marketplace-wide popularity list, so nothing
      about anyone's history reaches the response.
    end note
    S -> DAO : trending(limit, {}, viewerId = null)
    activate DAO
    DAO -> DB : SELECT … bid_count over trending_window_days\nWHERE status_id = 1 AND moderation_state = 'active'\n  AND date_end > now()\nORDER BY bid_count DESC, date_end ASC LIMIT ?
    DB --> DAO : rows
    DAO --> S : List<SearchResultItem> tagged Reason.TRENDING
    deactivate DAO

else registered buyer — full pipeline
    S -> DAO : recommendForUser(userId, limit)
    activate DAO

    DAO -> DB : SELECT auction_id FROM dismissed_recommendations WHERE user_id = ?
    DB --> DAO : dismissed ids (SCRUM-74)
    DAO -> DAO : pool = limit × 2 + |dismissed|

    == Stage 1 of 4: candidate generation (each generator fetches `pool`) ==

    note right of DAO
      The four stages used to run in sequence, each
      filling the slots the previous one left, so the
      final order was a function of stage boundaries.
      They are now <b>candidate generators</b>: none is
      told what an earlier one already took, because an
      auction several signals agree on has to reach the
      re-ranker from all of them.
    end note

    group PEER_BIDS — item-based CF (Component.CF)
        DAO -> DB : WITH my_items (bids ∪ watchlist),\n  peers (bid/watch my_items),\n  cand (COUNT DISTINCT peer per auction)\nSELECT … ORDER BY score DESC, date_end ASC LIMIT ?
        DB --> DAO : co-bidder candidates
    end

    group SIMILAR_TASTE — user-based CF (Component.UBCF)
        DAO -> DB : SELECT auction_id FROM auction\nWHERE status_id = 1 AND moderation_state = 'active'\n  AND date_end > now() AND seller_id <> ?
        DB --> DAO : eligibility allow-set
        note right of DAO
          <b>Regression fix (commit 01e1f47).</b> Ranking used to
          truncate to the top `limit` and let fetchItemsByIds
          apply these predicates afterwards, so a neighbourhood
          topped by closed auctions produced an <i>empty</i> arm
          rather than a shorter one. The allow-set is pushed
          down into ranking instead. Over-fetching a multiple of
          `limit` was rejected: it only moves the cliff.
        end note
        DAO -> DB : SELECT user_id, auction_id, age_days FROM bids\nUNION ALL … watchlist UNION ALL … browse_history
        DB --> DAO : every user's interaction rows
        DAO -> DAO : w = w_type × exp(-Δdays / recency_tau_days)\nkeep MAX decayed weight per (user, auction)
        DAO -> UBCF : rankAuctionIds(userId, vectors, pool,\n  dismissed, similarity_threshold, eligible)
        activate UBCF
        UBCF -> UBCF : cosine(target, peer) for every peer;\ndrop peers below similarity_threshold
        UBCF -> UBCF : score(i) = Σ sim(u,v) · w_vi\nover eligible, non-excluded items
        UBCF --> DAO : ranked auction ids
        deactivate UBCF
        DAO -> DB : SELECT … WHERE auction_id IN (…) AND still open
        DB --> DAO : hydrated candidates
    end

    group SAME_CATEGORY — content-based (Component.CONTENT)
        DAO -> DB : WITH my_signals (bids/watchlist/browse\n  inside content_window_days),\n  my_cats, my_tags\nSELECT … WHERE category ∈ my_cats OR tag ∈ my_tags\nORDER BY date_end ASC LIMIT ?
        DB --> DAO : content candidates + category_match / shared_tag
    end

    group TRENDING — popularity (Component.POPULARITY)
        DAO -> DB : SELECT … bid_count over trending_window_days,\nexcluding the viewer's own listings and items\nthey already bid on or watch
        DB --> DAO : trending candidates
    end

    == Stage 2 of 4: single re-ranking pass over the union ==

    DAO -> DAO : collect(): union by auction_id; each stage's\nwithin-stage rank (n−i)/n becomes that\ncomponent's raw score; every stage's own\nRecommendationProvenance is retained
    DAO -> DAO : fillRecencyComponent(): raw = t_latest_end − t_end\n(auction urgency, not history freshness)
    DAO -> DAO : min-max normalise each component across the\ncandidate set (all-zero if absent, all-one if flat)

    note right of DAO
      <b>score(i) =</b>
      ( w_cf·ĉf + w_ubcf·ûb + w_content·ĉt
        + w_pop·p̂op + w_rec·r̂ec )
      ÷ Σ w over components carrying any signal
      Weights are admin-tunable rows of
      recommendation_settings, not constants.
      All five zero ⇒ fall back to stage order.
    end note

    DAO -> DAO : labelWithStrongestArm(): reasonCode = the arm whose\nweighted contribution was largest (commit 5373d43),\nnot whichever generator ran first
    DAO -> DAO : why.setScore(score); why.setDominantComponent(…)\nRECENCY can dominate the score while having no arm
    DAO -> DAO : sort by score desc; ties → stage order, stage index, id

    == Stage 3 of 4: category diversity cap ==

    DAO -> DAO : capPerCategory(): at most ceil(limit / diversity_category_divisor)\nper category; items held back are appended once the capped\npass runs out, so the strip is never shorter
    DAO --> S : List<SearchResultItem> (≤ limit)
    deactivate DAO
end

== Stage 4 of 4: provenance, then the response ==

S -> DAO : attachProvenance(results, userId)
activate DAO
DAO -> DB : SELECT keyword FROM search_history WHERE user_id = ?\nGROUP BY keyword ORDER BY MAX(created_at) DESC LIMIT 12
DB --> DAO : the viewer's own recent keywords
DAO -> DAO : a card matching one is upgraded to SEARCH_KEYWORD,\ncarrying its score and dominantComponent across
DAO -> DB : SELECT auction_id, source_keyword, COUNT(*) … GROUP BY …
DB --> DAO : aggregate keywords per auction
DAO -> DB : SELECT auction_id, COUNT(*) AS clicks,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS clickers … WHERE event_type = 'CLICK'
DB --> DAO : click aggregates
DAO -> DB : SELECT DISTINCT ON (auction_id) … JOIN users
DB --> DAO : most recent clicker per auction
note right of DAO
  <b>Privacy split (PDPA).</b> Only aggregates and
  SecurityUtil.maskUsername output leave here, and a
  masked name is withheld until MIN_CLICKERS_TO_NAME = 2
  distinct people have clicked, so a lone clicker cannot
  be identified by elimination. Per-user rows are served
  only by /api/recommendations/attribution, behind
  requireRole("ADMIN").
end note
DAO --> S : items enriched in place
deactivate DAO

S -> S : personalised = isPersonalised(results)
note right of S
  Derived from the reason codes actually present
  (SEARCH_KEYWORD / PEER_BIDS / SIMILAR_TASTE /
  SAME_CATEGORY), <b>not</b> from having a session. A
  signed-in account with no history falls through to
  trending filler and is honestly reported as false.
end note
S -> B : 200 { results, personalised, trendingWindowDays }
deactivate S

B -> B : heading + subtitle chosen from the landing_content\nrows (admin-editable): "Recommended for You" when\npersonalised, else "Popular Right Now" + a prompt to\nbid or watch
B -> U : Recommendation strip\n("why this?" per card: reason, match score,\ndominant component, click count, keywords)

== Feedback loop: impressions and clicks populate recommendation_events ==

B -> B : unseenThisSession(): drop {auctionId}:{reasonCode} pairs\nalready recorded in sessionStorage this session\n(no viewport check — see PTD §3.9.9)
B -> S : POST /api/recommendations/events\ntype=impression, auctionIds=CSV, reasonCode=<arm>\n(one request per arm present in the batch)
activate S
S -> DAO : recordEvent(userId, id, "IMPRESSION", keyword, reasonCode)
activate DAO
DAO -> DAO : normaliseReasonCode(): only Reason enum names plus\nTRENDING_CONTROL are stored; anything a client\ninvented is dropped
DAO -> DB : INSERT INTO recommendation_events\n(user_id, auction_id, event_type, source_keyword, reason_code)
note right of DAO
  Best-effort and column-tolerant: source_keyword and
  reason_code arrived in later migrations, so the write
  is retried with fewer columns rather than lost.
  Analytics must never break the page.
end note
DAO --> S : (ignored)
deactivate DAO
S -> B : 200 Recorded.
deactivate S

U -> B : Click a recommended card
B -> S : POST /api/recommendations/events\ntype=click, auctionId, keyword, reasonCode
note right of B
  Opening "why this?" or dismissing a card is not a
  click-through — both are excluded in onClickCapture.
  The lower Trending strip posts reasonCode =
  TRENDING_CONTROL, giving the personalised arms a
  non-personalised baseline to be read against.
end note
activate S
S -> DAO : recordEvent(… "CLICK" …)
DAO -> DB : INSERT INTO recommendation_events
S -> B : 200 Recorded.
deactivate S

note over U, DB
  <b>Per-arm measurement.</b> AdminApiServlet GET /api/admin/recommendations
  (ADMIN only) groups these rows by reason_code into impressions, clicks,
  CTR and bids-after-click. Rows written before commit e672746 carry a NULL
  reason_code and are excluded rather than folded into an arm.
  <b>Not an A/B test</b>: nobody is randomised, and the two strips sit at
  different heights on the same page, so position bias structurally favours
  the recommendation strip. See PTD §3.9.9 and §3.9.10.
end note

@enduml
